# [[ project_name ]]

Read [README.md](README.md). Edit the feature in `src/[[ package_name ]]/` and its tests; use the public `heinzel`
and `heinzel.kernel` API. One `@module.operation` supplies CLI, MCP and REST. Add a service or model when the
feature needs it; neither is required for a small operation. Never import a plugin implementation or core internals.

Run from this plugin's directory:
```sh
make setup
make check-fast
make check
make build
make acceptance
make preview
make lock # after changing dependencies
make release # preview only
```
[% if layout == 'collection' %]
Keep all feature changes in this directory. Setup never initializes Git for a collection plugin. The owning
Makefile works even when the parent repository builds something else. A parent that provides collection commands
also accepts `NAME=[[ project_name ]]` from its root. Publication uses the parent's generic plugin release workflow;
do not copy a nested `.github/` or edit a parent registry.
[% endif %]

Each pytest file declares exactly one level: `unit`, `integration` or `e2e`. Write a behavior test for a change.
Run the fast loop while editing and the owning full/installed-wheel checks before release. These commands install
a compatible core wheel; they never run the core suite. `CORE=<wheel>` explicitly selects a candidate SDK.

The source operation in the starter shows the minimum abstraction. Use the contributions preset or
[extension guide](https://github.com/helmut-hoffer-von-ankershoffen/heinzel/blob/main/docs/PLUGINS.md) for views,
priorities, Today widgets, notifications and shared contracts. Ordinary dependencies belong in this project's
`pyproject.toml`; regenerate its own lock. Use conventional commits and review nontrivial changes.

Publishing requires a merged commit, this plugin's version and configured GitHub/PyPI publishing. Local
`release` only checks/builds and previews; `publish` dispatches the protected workflow. Never upload a different
rebuild under an existing version. See the README's release steps.
Private sources use private GitHub releases and the installing person's `gh` login. PyPI is public; do not publish
private sources there. The [custom plugin guide](https://github.com/helmut-hoffer-von-ankershoffen/heinzel/blob/main/docs/PLUGIN_AUTHORING.md)
documents separate repositories, catalog discovery and both release paths.
